'''top-timber''' (top′tim″bėr), ''n.'' ''Naut.'', one of the uppermost timbers in the side of a vessel.—{{smaller|'''Long top-timber''', the timber above each of the first futtocks.—'''Short top-timber''', the timber above each of the second futtocks.}}

'''top-tool''' (top′töl), ''n.'' A forging-tool resembling a cold-chisel or a short thick spike, held when in use by means of a flexible handle of hazel-wood or wire. When its cutting edge is round it is called a ''top-fuller''.

'''toquake†''', ''v. t.'' [ME. ''toquaken''; < ''to-''<sup>2</sup> + ''quake''.] To quake exceedingly. ''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 2527.

'''toquash†''', ''v. t.'' [ME. ''toquasshen''; < ''to-''<sup>2</sup> + ''quash''<sup>1</sup>.] To beat or crush to pieces. ''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 629.

'''toque''' (tōk), ''n.'' [< F. ''toque'' (= Sp. ''toca'' = Pg. ''touca'' = It. ''tocca''), a hat, bonnet, prob. < Bret. ''tok'' = W. ''toc'', hat, bonnet.] 1. A head-covering formerly worn by men and women—a diminished form of the hat with turned-up brim. {{smaller|It gradually approached the shape of a very small light cap of silk,}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller|which was surrounded and compressed by a band of twisted silk, or of richer material, in such a way as to give it a slight resemblance to a hat with a brim. Its complete form was reached about 1560. It was generally adorned with a small plume.}}

{{smaller block|The Swisse in black velvet ''toques'', led by 2 gallant cavalieres habited in scarlet-colour'd sattin.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Sept. 7, 1651.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ordinary head-dress [at Lha' Ssa] is a blue ''toque'', with a wide rim of black velvet, surmounted with a red knot. ''Huc'', Travels (trans. 1852), II. 149.}}

{{smaller block|His velvet ''toque'' stuck . . . upon the side of his head.
{{right|''Motley''. (''Imp. Dict.'')}}}}

2. A small bonnet in the shape of a round, close-fitting crown without a projecting brim, worn by women in the nineteenth century.

{{smaller block|Her delicate head, sculpturesquely defined by its ''toque''.
{{right|''Howells'', Indian Summer, ii.}}}}

3. The bonnet-macaque, ''Macacus sinensis'', so called from the arrangement of the hairs of the head into a kind of toque or cap; also, some similar monkey, as ''M. pileolatus'' of Ceylon. See cut under ''bonnet-macaque''.—4. A small nominal money of account, used in trading on some parts of the west coast of Africa.

{{smaller block|Forty cowries make one toque, and five toques one hen or gallinha. ''Simmonds''.}}

